== 4. WHAT A HUMAN MUST VERIFY AFTER APPLYING ==

  1. THE POLICY DOCUMENT'S REAL SHAPE. Open it and read how a classification and
     its egress set are spelled. The two rows in section 3.1 carry the right
     VALUES; their nesting and key names must match the document's own. If the
     egress set is spelled with a different key, use that key.

  2. THE FAMILY KEY, SUBSTITUTED FROM THE DOCUMENT. Replace every
     <FAMILY_CLASSIFICATION> in sections 3.1, 3.2 and 3.4 with the key the policy
     document actually spells. If you substitute it wrongly, the assertion and
     the third test pass against a tier that does not exist, which is the worst
     available outcome: a guard reporting success about nothing.

  3. THE FAMILY ROW, READ WITH YOUR OWN EYES, BEFORE AND AFTER. Not the test
     result: the row. This is the one item on this list that is not delegable to
     the suite.

  4. THE FIVE TIER NAMES ARE SPELLED AS THIS FILE SPELLS THEM. Section 3.4
     enumerates them. If any differs in the target repository, the second test
     silently checks fewer tiers than it appears to, which is a test that passes
     for the wrong reason. Fix the spelling; do not narrow the list.

  5. WHATEVER ELSE ENUMERATES TARGETS OR TIERS. A schema, a validator, a
     documentation table, an operator note. Adding a target in two places and
     forgetting a third is how a validator starts refusing a target the policy
     permits.

  6. THE BUS IS STILL UNREACHABLE FROM OUTSIDE. This change makes the bus a
     legitimate destination for two tiers. It does not, and must not, make the
     bus reachable from the internet: it stays on the internal network with no
     published port and no proxy route. Adding an egress target is a policy
     change, not a networking one.

  7. NOTHING IN EITHER FILE NAMES A PARTICULAR. No host, no address, no port, no
     storage identifier, no numeric messaging identifier, no figure. The two rows
     above contain a target name and two prose descriptions.

